      "'We helped a client achieve incredible results!' is the agency hook that makes people assume you're about to sell them something, and they're right, so they scroll past before you even get to the actual proof sitting three sentences later. Agency owners have real, specific results — a CAC cut in half, a launch that outperformed every projection, a client relationship saved after a rocky first quarter — and keep opening every case study with the same three words of ad copy instead of the actual story underneath it. It's a strange habit for people whose entire job is knowing that specificity sells and vagueness doesn't, but agency owners write their own marketing the way everyone writes their own marketing: rushed, generic, and squeezed in between actual client work. The case study itself is usually great. The hook in front of it is the reason nobody reads far enough to find out. CarouseLabs writes hooks that read like a story got interrupted mid-sentence, not a pitch that just started, using the real number or moment instead of the adjective everyone in the industry already reaches for. It's the same discipline agencies apply to every client's copy, finally pointed at their own — because a case study that reads like ad copy earns exactly the skepticism ad copy deserves, and a genuinely good result deserves better than getting buried under three words a prospect has already learned to ignore.",
