    hookIntro:
      "'Grab your copy today!' is a hook that works reasonably well for selling phone cases and falls flat for selling a book someone spent years of actual life writing, because a phone case asks for thirty seconds of decision-making and a book asks for hours of someone's genuinely scarce attention — the two demand completely different kinds of persuasion, and retail urgency language is built for the wrong one entirely. Authors default to retail-style hooks anyway because that's the language marketing has borrowed from everywhere else, and because after years spent inside the actual writing, describing the book from the outside in a way that earns curiosity feels almost impossibly hard — you know the book too well to remember what it feels like not knowing what happens next, the exact problem a stranger scrolling past your post is starting from. The book itself usually has a genuinely compelling hook buried somewhere inside it: a line, a premise, a question the whole story circles around, and it rarely makes it into the marketing copy because marketing copy defaults to urgency and availability instead of the actual curiosity the book itself is built to create in any reader right from page one, well before even a single word of any actual, explicit sales pitch has ever been made directly to them. CarouseLabs writes hooks that ask for curiosity instead of an impulse buy, pulling the compelling premise or line out of the book itself rather than defaulting to retail language borrowed from a completely different kind of product. The book was never the problem — years of work rarely are — the marketing copy sitting in front of it was, and closing that gap usually just takes pulling the one true, specific detail that made you want to write the thing in the first place back out into the open.",
