    realExample:
      "A course creator types: 'Modules I haven't covered: pricing psychology, objection handling, and client onboarding.' CarouseLabs returns a 3-topic plan: a pricing-psychology teaching preview built around one specific, counterintuitive pricing principle from the module, an objection-handling technique preview walking through one real reframe students learn to use, and an onboarding-process preview showing the first step of the module's onboarding checklist — spreading coverage across the actual curriculum instead of repeating the same familiar opening module. A prospective buyer who happens to see all three posts over the course of a week walks away with a genuinely fuller sense of what they'd be paying for than any single sales page could convey on its own.\n\nA second course creator, running a technical skills course, types: 'This week: want to preview the debugging module and the deployment module, both usually skipped in my marketing because they're less flashy than the intro project.' CarouseLabs builds a 2-topic plan: a debugging-module preview centered on one specific bug-hunting technique that students consistently rate as the most useful skill in the whole course, and a deployment-module preview walking through one step of getting a first project live, deliberately choosing the \"unglamorous\" modules precisely because they're the ones prospective students most underestimate the value of before enrolling. Previewing exactly the modules a buyer would otherwise assume are filler tends to be the strongest possible rebuttal to that assumption.\n\nChoosing the \"boring\" modules deliberately, rather than the flashy opening one, tends to do more to convince a skeptical buyer that the course delivers on substance, not just a strong sales pitch.\n\nA third course creator, whose course reviews consistently mention one specific mid-course module as the turning point, types: 'Module 6 keeps coming up in reviews as the one that actually clicked for people, but I've never made a dedicated post about it.' CarouseLabs turns that into a 2-post plan: a preview post built around the specific concept from module six that reviews keep citing, and a review-spotlight post pairing an actual (anonymized) student quote about that module with the preview, which is a considerably stronger pitch than a generic \"check out my course\" post ever manages to be.\n\nA fourth course creator, running a cohort-based program rather than a self-paced course, types: 'This week: want to preview the live Q&A module and the peer-feedback module, both usually overshadowed in my marketing by the flashier recorded-lesson content.' CarouseLabs builds a 2-topic plan: a live-Q&A preview post capturing one genuinely surprising question a past cohort asked and how it was answered, and a peer-feedback preview post explaining how the structured feedback process works, both aimed at the exact hesitation cohort-based programs face most — whether the community and interaction are worth the premium over a cheaper self-paced alternative.",
