    hookIntro:
      "Being an author involves research, drafting, editing, and talking to readers, and most author content only ever covers one of those — usually \"buy my book\" — while the other three, often more interesting parts of the actual writing life, go completely undocumented, as if the book simply materialized rather than being built decision by decision over months or years. The research rabbit hole you fell into on Tuesday, chasing down one obscure historical detail for a single paragraph in chapter twelve, is genuinely fascinating and nobody outside your own head will ever hear about it. The editing decision you made on Thursday, cutting a scene you'd spent three weeks writing because it slowed the pacing, says more about how you actually think as a writer than any promotional post ever could, and it's the exact kind of decision most authors never explain to anyone outside their own head. And the interesting question a reader asked you in a DM last week just sits there, unanswered publicly, even though a hundred other readers probably wonder the exact same thing. Readers who genuinely love an author's work are often more curious about how the sausage gets made than authors assume, and a feed that only ever says \"buy my book\" quietly wastes that curiosity instead of feeding it. CarouseLabs builds a week that covers the parts of being a writer that aren't just promotion, so the actual texture of the writing life — curious, messy, occasionally obsessive — becomes visible instead of staying hidden behind a steady drumbeat of buy-my-book posts.",
