    realExample:
      "An author types: 'This week: researching a historical detail for chapter 12, editing chapter 4, got an interesting reader question.' CarouseLabs returns a 3-topic plan: a research-rabbit-hole post about the historical detail — how one throwaway line in the book led to two full days of reading about 19th-century shipping routes — an editing-decision post about what changed in chapter 4 and why, without spoiling the plot, and a reader-question response post, treating the question as worth a real public answer rather than a private DM reply. Readers following along get a small window into a process most authors keep entirely to themselves until the finished book eventually appears on a shelf.\n\nNone of the three posts required any polish beyond what a normal week of thinking about the book already produced on its own.\n\nA second author, deep in drafting a sequel with an established readership, types: 'This week: cut a subplot I'd been attached to for months, and got a reader message asking if a specific side character survives.' CarouseLabs builds a 2-topic plan: an honest post on cutting the beloved subplot, walking through the reasoning without revealing what replaced it, which readers of ongoing series tend to find genuinely reassuring rather than alarming, and a playful non-answer post to the survival question, acknowledging it without spoiling anything, which usually generates more speculative comments than a straight answer ever would.\n\nA third author, working on a debut nonfiction book, types: 'This week: fact-checking a claim in chapter 2 turned up a contradiction in my sources, had to figure out which source to trust.' CarouseLabs turns that into a 2-post plan: a fact-checking post walking through how conflicting sources were weighed against each other, genuinely useful and interesting to readers who care about nonfiction credibility, and a broader post on how much fact-checking actually goes into a single chapter of nonfiction, which most readers dramatically underestimate.\n\nA fourth author, self-publishing a second book after a first release that underperformed expectations, types: 'This week: rewriting the opening chapter for the third time, and reflecting on what I'd actually do differently after book one's slow launch.' CarouseLabs builds a 2-topic plan: a drafting post on what specifically wasn't working about the first two versions of the opening chapter, and an honest reflection post on the lessons from book one's launch, which tends to resonate strongly with the large audience of aspiring and early-career authors who rarely hear candid post-mortems from someone who's actually lived through a underwhelming first launch.",
